A6xx GPU work-period accounting test
==================================

Backported from Qualcomm KGSL at commit
f374d957ba30cd3107f1ab007712e7f27b07def9:
https://github.com/qualcomm-linux/kgsl/tree/f374d957ba30cd3107f1ab007712e7f27b07def9
See kgsl.c, kgsl_drawobj.c, kgsl_power_trace.h, adreno_dispatch.c and
adreno_a6xx_ringbuffer.c. This device uses the older unified ringbuffer path.

The counters come from CP_ALWAYS_ON_CONTEXT, not CPU submission latency.
A6xx context ticks exclude time spent preempted. Per-UID totals use the
upstream 19.2 MHz conversion and 900 ms reporting interval. Secure and
fault-replayed work is excluded. Reset/stale counter pairs are discarded.
The profile buffer grows to 8 KiB to retain its previous entry capacity.
Non-A6xx GPUs do not acquire an invented active-time estimate.

Adaptations: reporting references are released after leaving the UID list
lock, without coalescing deferred puts; teardown stops and drains reporting;
failed process initialization releases accounting state. Existing kernel
and vendor thermal/power policies are unchanged.

Host lifetime/arithmetic check (mock kernel primitives, ASan and UBSan):
  python3 tools/testing/selftests/kgsl/test_work_period.py

Device validation, after building and booting BOTH backports:
1. Run the BPF test described in ../bpf/README.atomics as root.
2. Copy collect_gpu_accounting.sh to /data/local/tmp, then run as root:
     sh /data/local/tmp/collect_gpu_accounting.sh
   Scroll and switch between two apps while it captures. This uses a private
   ftrace instance and does not clear the system trace buffer.
3. Pull /data/local/tmp/gpu-accounting to the host and run:
     python3 tools/testing/selftests/kgsl/check_work_period_trace.py \
       gpu-accounting/work-period.trace
4. In logcat, confirm gpuWork.bpf no longer fails loading. Check bpf-paths.txt
   for GPU program/maps. Nonzero ftrace events alone do not prove BPF attached.
5. Repeat with a GPU-heavy app, app exit, lock/unlock and suspend/resume.
   Check dmesg for GPU faults/page faults and KASAN/lockdep reports when enabled.
   Verify protected video playback still works. Compare workload and idle
   battery behavior with the previous kernel.

Expected trace layout: gpu_id offset 8, uid 12, start_time_ns 16,
end_time_ns 24, total_active_duration_ns 32. Periods for the same GPU/UID must
not overlap, active time must be positive and <= the period, and the period
must be <= 1 second. The validator checks these constraints. Kernel worker
starvation can delay the upstream timer/work reporting scheme; a period over
1 second or missing events is a failed device test, not something to ignore.

This is a backport candidate for separate kernel testing, not a claim of
hardware validation. Full kernel compilation was unavailable on the macOS
host (kernel configuration tool requires Linux features.h).
